# Integrate an owned component

Adapted from siminfeng/c2/skills/engine-build/SKILL.md and c2/METHODS.md.
The old ref-tree/build-CC/jobq shell sequence is superseded here by the owned
agent checkout, factory inspector and asynchronous factory.builder capsules.
The host enforces authority; this material grants none. Commit only when the
operator has authorized committing; otherwise publish the pending diff and stop
before a builder request that requires clean committed source.

1. Query/read current evidence and the decision revision. Read the component
   manifest, verify both copied patch hashes, base commit and combined-patch
   provenance. Work only in the owned checkout under FACTORY_WORKSPACE, never
   in siminfeng reference trees. Pre-integration e14b87603 already includes
   combined-01..08; never reapply it. Preserve unrelated changes.
2. Run git status and verify HEAD. For the candidate patch first run
   `git apply --reverse --check PATCH`. If it succeeds, record already applied
   and never apply twice. Otherwise run `git apply --check PATCH`, then apply
   once. A failed forward check is rejection evidence, not a reason to force.
   Publish the check output, patch SHA and diff as artifacts/observations.
3. Review the exact diff. When authorized, commit only the component change
   with a message citing the component SHA-256 from component.yaml (the patch
   sha256), retaining that manifest as evidence. Publish commit/tree identity.
4. Call factory.engine_inspector.inspect with source=the workspace handle and
   subpath=repository-relative path; supply the image when relevant. Read its
   observation and artifact. Require a clean source and the expected reverse
   patch detection. Check nvcc on the build worker's PATH, architecture and L4T.
5. Record the next decision and call factory.builder.submit with request and
   a fresh idem_key. Request source.path + source.commit, target jetson-orin,
   a unique factory/llamacpp tag and explicit deadline/environment. Submission
   acknowledges a queued attempt, never build success. Poll status/result in
   later bounded calls. Publish each transition/evidence reference. A retry
   uses a fresh attempt/output identity; never overwrite rejection evidence.
6. Build identity = commit + patch SHA + toolchain + base digest, together with
   build record/archive SHA. Distinguish local image Id from config/registry
   digest and tag. Binary hash alone is not build equivalence (METHODS D026).
   Preflight exact image/tool argv and effective config before lab submission.
   Runtime engagement and correctness are separate from build success.

Builds and scored GPU work must not overlap. Keep generated staging, archives
and live build output out of source control. No autonomous push/deploy, clock,
power, driver or root-permission changes are part of this procedure.
# Before a bounded engine run

Adapted from siminfeng/cc/abrun/preflight.py, test_effective_config.py,
c2/METHODS.md and c2/MEASUREMENT-GAPS.md. Tag-keyed help caching in the old
preflight is superseded: resolve docker image inspect Id on every call, then
cache help by that immutable Id and tool under FACTORY_HOME/preflight/.
Retagging must never reuse help from the old image. Query /app/<tool> --help
using --runtime nvidia; reject every unsupported flag before a workload.

Call inspector.verify_effective_config with the exact image and LLAMA_EXPERT_*
string environment. It runs one CPU-only Granite token (-ngl 0, context 256),
with a 45-second subprocess deadline. This checks model-independent parsing,
not Qwen worker activation or performance. Unknown names and invalid values
must emit `expert_config: invalid NAME=VALUE (range R)` and abort: retain that
exact line, exit status and artifact. One effective line per context must match
all supplied values, even when fill/pregate are disabled. Never accept an OR
of activation regexes or a run label as configuration evidence. Read the
observation/artifact on CONFIG_REJECTED, CONFIG_MISMATCH or CONFIG_MISSING.

CUDA PATH lesson: nvcc installed on disk is insufficient. Check nvcc on the
actual build worker PATH and publish toolchain/version/flags. Pass required
PATH/CUDA settings before submission so the durable worker inherits them.
Build identity includes toolchain and digest-pinned base, not just source.
Older shells may need DOCKER_CMD_PREFIX='sg docker -c'; the complete Docker
argv must be one shell-quoted argument. Do not change group/root configuration.

llama-server memory lesson: inspect exact-image help and pin --cache-ram and
context/checkpoint behavior explicitly when supported. Defaults can reserve
large prompt/context-checkpoint caches and exhaust shared Jetson DRAM. Treat
unsupported --cache-ram as a preflight failure, never an ignored option. Record
actual allocation/headroom and checkpoint/cache settings; CPU/GPU RSS and
shared-DRAM counters overlap. Do not use Docker --memory as a Tegra CUDA cap.
The inspector config probe uses llama-completion and does not run a server.

Controller environment: set PYTHONPATH to the repository root so the host-sealed
Python interpreter can import the factory API (or install the package in that
interpreter). Set FACTORY_HOME, FACTORY_TASK_ID (existing task), and
FACTORY_WORKSPACE; use a host-bound workspace handle for inspect. Optional
FACTORY_READ_TASKS controls evidence reads, DOCKER_CMD_PREFIX wraps Docker,
and FACTORY_DOCKER is an offline fake executable argv only for tests. Native
capsules inherit environment. Supply process.exec, plus workspace.read for
inspect or gpu.use for NVIDIA-runtime probes, via operator grants.
# Repair from evidence

Adapted from siminfeng/c2/skills/engine-ab-experiment/SKILL.md, c2/METHODS.md
and c2/MEASUREMENT-GAPS.md. The old static spec/jobq driver instructions are
superseded by the factory decision/evidence and builder/lab interfaces.

Read the actual rejection: evidence.query returns bounded summaries/cursor;
evidence.read(id) returns the record or artifact preview. Follow the source
artifact ids. Do not diagnose from an arm name, submission acknowledgement,
missing metric interpreted as zero, or an old success. Preserve failed evidence.

Form a falsifiable hypothesis with uncertainty and expected discriminating
evidence. Reconsider any assumption contradicted by evidence: in particular,
requested K is not proof of effective K, a switch regex is not a value check,
and thread names do not establish quality or speed. Change exactly one thing
per repair. Record a new decision using expected_rev, evidence_ids and a
reason_for_change citing those evidence ids before the next bounded action.
A stale revision requires rereading the current decision, not overwriting it.

Never edit a scored contract. A changed contract needs a new revision and a
new comparable run set; it cannot repair an existing scored result. Keep
prompt/model/greedy/offload/context identities and baseline fixed. Distinguish
CPU config probes, activation evidence, byte-identical output correctness,
full-engine performance, simulation, and negative/null results. An absent
measurement has an unavailable reason; do not fabricate it. Sampled telemetry
has phase/window and contamination limits (MEASUREMENT-GAPS §§2–6).

Stop on exhausted task budget/deadline, authority or ownership uncertainty,
unsafe headroom, immutable-contract conflict, repeated rejection with no new
hypothesis, missing required evidence, or the decision's explicit stop condition.
Cancel only owned attempts/containers. Publish the stop reason and remaining
uncertainty. Never widen grants, force a patch, change the baseline to get a
win, or silently retry under the same evidence identity.
